WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.878
To fly from the Earth to Mars, you select two
dates: a launch date and an arrival date.

2
00:00:08.918 --> 00:00:12.826
On the launch date, the spacecraft
starts at the position of the Earth.

3
00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:16.737
On the arrival date, it must
be at the position of Mars.

4
00:00:16.937 --> 00:00:23.341
For less than one revolution, only one orbit around
the Sun connects these two positions in this time.

5
00:00:23.381 --> 00:00:29.266
To find this orbit is the problem of Lambert.
Apsis solves it with the method of Izzo.

6
00:00:29.666 --> 00:00:32.759
A different arrival date gives a different orbit.

7
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.585
Very fast flights use much energy. Very
slow flights can also use much energy.

8
00:00:39.985 --> 00:00:46.492
Now look at the departure. The Earth moves
around the Sun at 30.0 kilometers per second.

9
00:00:46.532 --> 00:00:52.857
The transfer orbit starts with a different
velocity: 33.0 kilometers per second.

10
00:00:52.897 --> 00:00:59.278
The difference of the two vectors is the speed at
infinity, v-infinity. The launch vehicle must give it.

11
00:00:59.318 --> 00:01:02.965
The square of v-infinity is the launch energy, C3.

12
00:01:03.565 --> 00:01:06.776
Now calculate C3 for many pairs of dates.

13
00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:14.957
Each point of this map is one transfer. The horizontal axis
is the launch date. The vertical axis is the arrival date.

14
00:01:14.997 --> 00:01:21.113
Bright colors show a low energy. Because of its
shape, the name of this map is porkchop plot.

15
00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:32.406
The lowest C3 is 9.18 square kilometers per second squared:
a launch on 31 October 2026 and a flight of 292 days.

16
00:01:32.806 --> 00:01:42.142
Along this line, the transfer angle is 180 degrees. There, the plane
of the transfer must turn very much, and the energy is very high.

17
00:01:42.182 --> 00:01:48.815
Thus the map has two parts: flights of less than half a
revolution, and flights of more than half a revolution.

18
00:01:49.414 --> 00:01:56.079
The good dates come again after each synodic
period. For Mars, this is approximately 26 months.

19
00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:02.324
This chart shows the lowest C3 for
each launch date from 2026 to 2035.

20
00:02:02.924 --> 00:02:09.726
In Apsis, the Transfer screen solves one pair of dates.
The Launch windows screen calculates the full map.
